<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cabira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">72 or 71 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cabira was fought near Cabira in Pontus, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>, in 72 or 71 BCE. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucius Licinius Lucullus</a>, after cutting the enemy supply lines, routed the army of Mithridates VI, who fled to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> control of most of Pontus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had already fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> twice. In the first war, after taking the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, he had massacred 80,000 Roman and other Italian civilians in the Asian Vespers. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv">Nicomedes IV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> died in 74 BCE and left his kingdom to Rome, Mithridates invaded it in 73 BCE, defeated the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius-cotta">Marcus Aurelius Cotta</a> and besieged him in Chalcedon. Lucullus, consul with Cotta and newly governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, took command of the Roman forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>Mithridates moved on Cyzicus, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but Lucullus trapped his army on the Cyzicus peninsula, starved it, and defeated his admiral Marcus Varius off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>. Of the 300,000 men who had set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, only 20,000 effective troops returned to Pontus. In 72 BCE, leaving Heraclea Pontica to Cotta, Lucullus marched through Galatia, whose people were glad to supply him, and let his troops plunder the Pontic heartland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mithridates rebuilt an army of 40,000 men, 4,000 of them cavalry, near Cabira. Lucullus suffered a setback in a first skirmish and had to withdraw, and further skirmishes followed, as well as an attempt on his life. His supplies now came north from Cappadocia, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Pontic cavalry attacked a convoy escorted by ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> under the legate Sornatius and suffered heavy losses.</p><p>Mithridates then sent some 4,000 cavalry and infantry against a second convoy under Marcus Fabius Hadrianus. In a narrow valley that hampered the horsemen, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> counter-attacked and destroyed half the force. The king tried to hide the scale of the defeat, but Hadrianus marched past his camp in full array with the spoils. Talk of retreat spread, and when Mithridates prepared to flee, his army fell apart. Lucullus attacked the fleeing troops, and the Romans took the camp, killed everyone left in it and began to loot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mithridates escaped nearly penniless. Before leaving he sent a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, to the royal palace to see to the deaths of his sisters, wives and concubines. Lucullus pressed the sieges across Pontus and organised it as a new Roman province, and sent Appius Claudius to demand that Tigranes hand Mithridates over. Tigranes refused and prepared for war, and in 69 BCE Lucullus marched his legions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cabira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Third Mithridatic War Lucullus broke his siege of Cyzicus in 73–72 BCE and defeated him at <b>Cabira</b>, and Pompey crushed his army on the Lycus river in 66 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
